<odoo>
    <data noupdate="1">
        <record id="group_ai_safety_reporting_service" model="res.groups">
            <!-- AI safety self-reporting service account, 2026-09-24. Full design context:
            docs/proposals/CHILD_SAFETY_COMMUNICATIONS_CONSENT.md (hams_com), section G, Phase 7
            item 1 ("bot self-reporting"). A safety-classification daemon
            (daemons/hams_simulated_bots, hams_com) runs an LLM pass over bot QSO transcripts
            and, when it flags self-harm, sexual content, or an uncivil interaction, files a
            hams_helpdesk.ticket (ticket_type='ai_safety_concern', see helpdesk_ticket.py) over
            the JSON-2 RPC API, authenticated as this account.

            Named "ai_safety_reporting", not "bot_reporting" or anything bot-specific: Phase 7
            item 2 (the "Official Observer" agent, a distinct feature, not built by this module)
            is expected to reuse this exact same reporting path later. Hardcoding "bot" into
            this identifier would misdescribe that second caller.

            Why privilege_helpdesk (not a new privilege category): reuses the existing
            hams_helpdesk.privilege_helpdesk category, the same reasoning hams_helpdesk's own
            group_ai_triage_external_service already gives for reusing it instead of inventing a
            new one (see hams_helpdesk/security/helpdesk_security.xml), and the same
            "privilege_helpdesk holds several non-hierarchical, narrowly-scoped groups side by
            side" precedent pager_duty/security/security.xml establishes for privilege_pager_duty.

            Why a brand-new group rather than reusing group_ai_triage_external_service: that
            group is READ-only and allow-list scoped for an external triage AI reading existing
            tickets; this account's job is the opposite shape (CREATE-only, never read, write, or
            unlink; it has no business seeing any ticket, including its own, once filed). Base
            internal-user status (base.group_user) is granted directly on the service-account
            user record below, not via implied_ids on this group, matching every other narrow
            service-account group in this codebase (group_ai_triage_external_service,
            pager_duty.user_pager_mcp_triage_service, and others). Deliberately NOT implied
            by/implying group_helpdesk_user or group_helpdesk_manager: those groups' own ir.rule
            (rule_helpdesk_ticket_user / rule_helpdesk_ticket_manager) would OR together with
            rule_helpdesk_ticket_ai_safety_reporting_creator below on the same model (Odoo
            ir.rule records for the SAME user are OR'ed, not AND'ed, across every group that user
            is in), which would silently defeat this account's category scoping the moment it
            also picked up either of those. -->
            <field name="name">AI Safety Reporting Service Account Group</field>
            <field name="privilege_id" ref="hams_helpdesk.privilege_helpdesk"/>
        </record>

        <record id="rule_helpdesk_ticket_ai_safety_reporting_creator" model="ir.rule">
            <!-- Defense-in-depth beyond the create-only ir.model.access grant (see
            ir.model.access.csv): an unconditional domain_force, matching pager_duty's own
            pager_incident_website_rule_creator precedent, WOULD be `[(1, '=', 1)]`. Instead,
            this codebase's own "narrowest useful grant" ethos (e.g. the CSAM allow-list rule in
            hams_helpdesk/security/helpdesk_security.xml) calls for scoping the domain to only
            the ticket_type values this account is meant to create, so it structurally cannot
            create any OTHER ticket_type even if a caller-side bug tried to pass one.
            Verified this is actually enforceable, not just declared: Odoo's ir.rule create-time
            check validates domain_force against the record's post-insert state (see
            test_service_account_cannot_create_a_general_ticket in
            tests/test_ai_safety_reporting.py, which proves both the positive create() case for
            ai_safety_concern and the negative case for 'general'/the default). Includes
            csam_enticement_trafficking alongside ai_safety_concern: this account's real-world
            caller, the safety-classification daemon, can flag any of the four
            SafetyClassification categories, and a genuine CSAM/enticement/trafficking flag must
            still be filed as _CSAM_TICKET_TYPE (hams_helpdesk's own mandatory NCMEC-reporting
            workflow, see helpdesk_ticket.py there) rather than folded into the general
            ai_safety_concern category.

            Known real gap, found 2026-09-24 by this module's own tests (see
            test_service_account_creating_a_csam_ticket_currently_fails_on_the_core_write_
            requirement in tests/test_ai_safety_reporting.py for the full account): this rule's
            domain DOES allow a csam_enticement_trafficking create for this account, but
            hams_helpdesk.ticket.create()'s own pre-existing NCMEC packet-assembly step still
            fails it with an unrelated AccessError, because that step writes to the ticket under
            the ambient (calling) identity rather than an elevated service account, and this is
            the first genuinely create-only caller of create() for this ticket_type. That is a
            hams_helpdesk-side gap, not a flaw in this rule's own domain: the domain is left as
            written (not narrowed to just ai_safety_concern) so that fixing the write requirement
            on the hams_helpdesk side is sufficient on its own, with no matching change needed
            here. -->
            <field name="name">Helpdesk Ticket AI Safety Reporting Creator</field>
            <field name="model_id" ref="hams_helpdesk.model_hams_helpdesk_ticket"/>
            <field name="domain_force">[('ticket_type', 'in', ['ai_safety_concern', 'csam_enticement_trafficking'])]</field>
            <field name="groups" eval="[(4, ref('group_ai_safety_reporting_service'))]"/>
        </record>
    </data>
    <data>
        <!-- AI Safety Reporting Service Account. Deliberately in this non-noupdate <data> block,
        not the noupdate="1" block above, so that a (6, 0, ...) group-membership fix on this
        record actually takes effect on an existing database when the module is upgraded (-u),
        matching hams_helpdesk.user_ai_triage_service's own comment in
        hams_helpdesk/security/helpdesk_security.xml for exactly why. -->
        <record id="user_ai_safety_reporting_service" model="res.users">
            <field name="name">AI Safety Reporting Service Account</field>
            <field name="login">ai_safety_reporting_service_internal</field>
            <field name="company_id" ref="base.main_company"/>
            <field name="company_ids" eval="[(4, ref('base.main_company'))]"/>
            <field name="notification_type">email</field>
            <field name="is_service_account" eval="True"/>
            <field name="active" eval="True"/>
            <field name="group_ids" eval="[(6, 0, [ref('group_ai_safety_reporting_service'), ref('base.group_user')])]"/>
        </record>
    </data>
</odoo>
